{# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Per-host silence detectors (os, hardware).

   One stanza per by_host splunk_docker_silence_detectors entry (see defaults
   for the field reference): tstats latest(_time) over the index BY HOST, fire
   when a host's own silence exceeds its cadence-derived threshold. Kept as
   its own stanza per detector rather than folded into index_gap_detector
   below (Zammad 17191) because per-host cadence needs one row per host, which
   a single cross-index report cannot express — a healthy emitter can't mask
   a dead one, and a host that has never emitted has no baseline and is not
   detected.

   Threshold is CADENCE-DERIVED, not the flat det.threshold_minutes: a host's
   own historical average inter-event gap (avg_gap_minutes, from
   count/earliest/latest over the same tstats) sets its effective threshold
   at cadence_multiplier x that gap, floored at det.threshold_minutes so a
   host with too little history to compute a gap still gets the configured
   default. A per-host `host_overrides` map (name -> minutes) pins an exact
   value when the computed baseline is wrong (e.g. a host too new to have a
   representative average yet). This exists because a single flat threshold
   across hosts of wildly different natural logging cadence fires
   continuously for the quiet-but-healthy ones — see docs/ or the PR that
   added this for the dry-run evidence.

   tstats returns ZERO rows (not a row with a null last_seen) when nothing in
   the whole lookback window matches — the llm_router lesson, verified live:
   coalesce(last_seen, 0) alone did not fix it, because there is no row for
   eval to apply it to. The appendpipe guard below synthesizes a sentinel row
   when the tstats result set is empty, and coalesce stays as a second guard
   once that row exists.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- #}
{% for det in splunk_docker_silence_detectors | default([]) if det.by_host | default(false) %}
[{{ det.name }}_silence_detector]
description = Alerts when the {{ det.index }} index has received no events for more than {{ det.threshold_minutes }} minutes{% if det.sourcetype is defined %} (sourcetype={{ det.sourcetype }}){% endif %}{% if det.by_host | default(false) %}, detected per-host against each host's own cadence so a healthy emitter cannot mask a dead one{% endif %}. Catches the emitter, its Cribl pipeline, and Splunk HEC ingestion failing with a single signal.
{# The appendpipe guard covers the fully-empty-index blind spot: tstats over
   an index/window with zero matching events returns ZERO rows — with by_host
   that means zero rows instead of a null-last_seen row per host; without
   by_host it means zero rows instead of one null-last_seen row. Either way,
   without the guard the alert goes quiet exactly when everything is dead.
   stats count on the empty set still yields one count=0 row (an ungrouped
   aggregate always emits a row), which synthesizes the sentinel result. #}
search = | tstats count as event_count latest(_time) as last_seen{% if det.by_host | default(false) %} earliest(_time) as first_seen{% endif %} WHERE index={{ det.index }}{% if det.sourcetype is defined %} sourcetype={{ det.sourcetype }}{% endif %}{% if det.by_host | default(false) %} by host | appendpipe [ stats count as _rows | where _rows == 0 | eval host = "(no hosts reporting)", last_seen = 0, first_seen = 0, event_count = 0 | fields - _rows ] | eval avg_gap_minutes = if(event_count > 1, (last_seen - first_seen) / 60 / (event_count - 1), null()) | eval host_threshold_minutes = case({% for host, mins in (det.host_overrides | default({})) | dictsort %}host="{{ host }}", {{ mins }}, {% endfor %}true(), max(coalesce(avg_gap_minutes, 0) * {{ det.cadence_multiplier | default(4) }}, {{ det.threshold_minutes }})){% else %} | appendpipe [ stats count as _rows | where _rows == 0 | eval last_seen = 0, event_count = 0 | fields - _rows ]{% endif %} | eval minutes_silent = (now() - coalesce(last_seen, 0)) / 60 | where minutes_silent > {% if det.by_host | default(false) %}host_threshold_minutes{% else %}{{ det.threshold_minutes }}{% endif +%}
{# The `+` above is load-bearing. Ansible renders templates with
   trim_blocks=True, which eats the newline that follows a `{% ... %}` block
   tag. This `search =` line ends in one, so without the `+` the comment block
   below is welded onto the end of the search value. Splunk then dispatches the
   search on schedule and it fails with a FATAL parse error every single time —
   "The operator at '# Lookback derives...' is invalid" — so the detector can
   never fire. That shipped, and it silently disabled 17 detectors at once.
   A bare jinja2 Environment defaults trim_blocks=False and reproduces none of
   this, which is why the tests must set it explicitly; see
   tests/templates/conftest.py. #}
# Lookback derives from this detector's own threshold (not a flat constant
# shared by every detector regardless of cadence): wide enough that a host
# silent for many multiples of its own threshold is still inside the window.
# tstats reads only indexed metadata, so a wide window is cheap. This does
# NOT make the window unbounded — a host silent longer than this still has no
# row for tstats to return at all (the same inherent gap the "zero events in
# the whole index" case above closes only for the fully-empty-index case).
# Closing that class fully needs comparing against the full host roster
# (an inventory-diff detector), not a wider tstats lookback; out of scope here.
dispatch.earliest_time = -{{ (det.threshold_minutes * splunk_docker_silence_lookback_multiplier) | int }}m
dispatch.latest_time = now
cron_schedule = {{ det.cron }}
enableSched = 1
is_visible = 1
disabled = {{ det.disabled | default(0) }}
alert.severity = 3
alert.suppress = 1
alert.suppress.period = {{ det.suppress_minutes }}m
{% if det.by_host | default(false) %}
# Scope suppression per host: without this, the first silent host suppresses
# the alert globally and a second host failing inside the window goes unseen.
alert.suppress.fields = host
{% endif %}
alert.track = 1
counttype = number of events
relation = greater than
quantity = 0

{% endfor %}
{# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   One gap detector, replacing the flat (non-by_host) per-index stanzas the
   loop above used to also emit (Zammad 17191: 18 hand-tuned stanzas, several
   admittedly firing every cycle over structurally-empty indexes -- the same
   information in one search instead of eighteen). Thresholds stay declarative
   in splunk_docker_silence_detectors; this only changes how they render.

   INDEX TIME, NOT EVENT TIME (Vikunja 3111): three indexes (claude, llm,
   mac_perf) currently report negative event age via a sourcetype-level
   clock-skew/parse defect -- their latest _time is in the future relative to
   the search head. A detector keyed on _time would read that as "very
   recently updated" and never fire even if the source went silent, exactly
   backwards. _indextime is Splunk's own receipt timestamp, set by the
   indexer at write time and immune to a sender's clock or parse config, so
   last_indexed below tracks when Splunk last WROTE a row for the index, not
   when the event claims to have happened. tstats aggregates _indextime the
   same way it aggregates _time -- both are default indexed metadata fields,
   not something a data model or acceleration is needed for.

   The append+stats max fills the same fully-empty-index blind spot the
   by_host loop above closes with appendpipe, but for a wildcarded search:
   tstats over index=* returns one row per index that has ANY event in the
   window, so an index with ZERO events in the whole window (never ingested,
   or dead longer than the window) has no row at all rather than a row with a
   stale last_indexed. Appending a synthetic last_indexed=0 row for every
   index this role declares and taking stats max(...) by index means a real
   tstats row wins where one exists (max picks the large real timestamp over
   the 0 sentinel) and the sentinel wins where none does -- age_min then
   comes out enormous, which is exactly "silent" for an index that has never
   said a word.

   threshold_minutes is a case() over the same per-index thresholds already
   declared in splunk_docker_silence_detectors, mirroring the host_overrides
   case() the by_host loop above already uses -- not a Splunk `| lookup`
   against a deployed CSV, which the design draft for this change proposed.
   A lookup needs its own transforms.conf stanza, its own file deployment
   task and its own test surface for a table this role already regenerates
   on every converge from data it already owns; case() gets the identical
   result with no new moving part. Anything not in the list falls back to
   coalesce's 1440-minute (24h) default, so a newly created index is covered
   from the day it exists rather than only once someone remembers to add an
   entry for it.

   The lookback window has to be wide enough that the widest configured
   threshold's own multiple of itself still falls inside it, same reasoning
   as the by_host loop's dispatch.earliest_time above -- computed once here
   from the widest flat threshold rather than per-stanza since this is one
   search covering every index.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- #}
{% set flat_detectors = [] %}
{% for det in splunk_docker_silence_detectors | default([]) if not (det.by_host | default(false)) %}
{% set _ = flat_detectors.append(det) %}
{% endfor %}
{% set gap_lookback_minutes = ((flat_detectors | map(attribute='threshold_minutes') | list + [1440]) | max) * splunk_docker_silence_lookback_multiplier %}
{# Each half defaults independently (never a combined-then-defaulted
   expression) so a caller that supplies neither -- every template test
   except the ones exercising this stanza specifically -- still renders,
   with an empty expected-index list rather than a Jinja UndefinedError. #}
{% set gap_expected_indexes = (splunk_docker_indexes | default((splunk_docker_indexes_core | default([])) + (splunk_docker_indexes_extra | default([])))) | map(attribute='name') | list %}
[index_gap_detector]
description = Alerts when any index has gone silent longer than its configured threshold (default 1440 minutes), using the index's own last-write time rather than event time so a clock-skewed or unparsed sourcetype cannot hide behind a false "recently updated" reading. Replaces the flat per-index silence detectors with one report over every index this role creates.
search = | tstats latest(_indextime) as last_indexed where index=* _index_earliest=-{{ gap_lookback_minutes }}m _index_latest=now by index | append [ | makeresults | eval index="{{ gap_expected_indexes | join(',') }}" | eval index=split(index, ",") | mvexpand index | eval last_indexed=0 ] | stats max(last_indexed) as last_indexed by index | eval age_min = round((now() - last_indexed) / 60) | eval threshold_minutes = case({% for det in flat_detectors %}index="{{ det.index }}", {{ det.threshold_minutes }}, {% endfor %}true(), 1440) | where age_min > threshold_minutes
dispatch.earliest_time = -{{ gap_lookback_minutes }}m
dispatch.latest_time = now
cron_schedule = */10 * * * *
enableSched = 1
is_visible = 1
disabled = 0
alert.severity = 3
alert.suppress = 1
alert.suppress.period = 60m
# Scope suppression per index: without this, the first silent index
# suppresses the alert globally and a second index going silent inside the
# window goes unseen -- same reasoning as alert.suppress.fields = host above.
alert.suppress.fields = index
alert.track = 1
counttype = number of events
relation = greater than
quantity = 0
